<h3>1. The website builds the email</h3>
<p><code>MailSender</code> (in <code>Components/Email</code>) has one method per kind of email. Each builds a dictionary of placeholder values (for example <code>{username}</code>, <code>{event_name}</code>, <code>{event_link}</code>, <code>{registration_link}</code>, <code>{password_reset_link}</code>, with dates in UK format), wraps any link in a <a asp-page="/Docs/Admin/Registration" asp-fragment="links-in-emails-callback-tokens">callback token</a>, and calls <code>MailRepository.StartEmailAsync</code> <em>without waiting</em> (fire-and-forget), so the page returns at once.</p>

<h3>2. The hand-over</h3>
<p><code>MailRepository</code>:</p>
<ul>
    <li>creates an <code>EmailId</code> (a GUID) and records it in the <code>EmailDeliveryTracker</code> as <em>Handing over</em>;</li>
    <li>works out the email app's address: <code>EmailApi:BaseUrl</code> from configuration (must start with <code>https://</code>; <code>appsettings.json</code> sets <code>https://api.forestchurches.co.uk</code>), otherwise the Key Vault secret <code>app-email-url</code>, otherwise <code>https://localhost:7291</code> in Development;</li>
    <li>creates a JWT (HS256, 10 minutes, unique <code>jti</code>) signed with the Key Vault secrets <code>jwt-token-securityKey</code>, <code>jwt-token-issuer</code> and <code>jwt-token-audience</code>;</li>
    <li>posts <code>{ Id, To, Subject, Body: "", Template: "./templates/&lt;name&gt;.html", Data }</code> to <code>POST /Email</code> with <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>;</li>
    <li>tries up to <strong>3 times</strong> with 30, 40 and 50-second timeouts. Redirects aren't followed automatically (that would drop the token); a single redirect is re-posted manually with the token;</li>
    <li>logs every step with the <code>EmailId</code>, and marks the tracker <em>Accepted</em> (shown as <em>Received</em>) or <em>Failed</em> (<em>Not received</em>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. The email app receives it</h3>
<p><code>POST /Email</code> (JWT required) rejects emails with no recipient (400), assigns an ID if none was sent, records the email in the <code>EmailStatusStore</code> as <em>Queued</em>, adds it to a static in-memory <code>ConcurrentQueue</code> and replies <code>{ id, status, receivedUtc, queueLength }</code>.</p>

<h3 id="when-emails-are-sent">4. When emails are sent</h3>
<p><code>POST /Email/send</code> empties the queue and sends each email on its own, so one bad email can't stop the rest:</p>
<ul>
    <li>success → <em>Sent</em> with the time;</li>
    <li>failure on attempt 1 or 2 → <em>Retrying</em>, put back in the queue for the next run;</li>
    <li>failure on attempt 3 → <em>Failed</em>, with the error kept.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing outside calls <code>/Email/send</code>. Two hosted services inside the email app do:</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>EmailSenderHostedService</code> loops forever: create a JWT signed with <code>jwt-token-securityKey</code> (issuer <code>ForestChurches</code>, subject <code>partnerApplication</code>), call its own <code>{app-email-url}/Email/send</code>, wait 5 minutes.</li>
    <li><code>EmailScheduler</code> runs a timer every 5 minutes that calls the same service's loop. Because that loop never returns, each tick starts <em>another</em> never-ending loop, so the number of send loops grows by one every 5 minutes until the app restarts. The queue is thread-safe so emails aren't sent twice, but it wastes resources and log space; see <a asp-page="/Docs/Admin/KnownIssues">Known issues</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>So an email normally goes out within 5 minutes of being received.</p>

<h3>5. Building and sending the message</h3>
<p><code>EmailSender</code> reads the template file named in <code>Template</code> (relative to the app's folder, so <code>./templates/…</code>), replaces every placeholder key in <code>Data</code> with its value, and sends it with MailKit:</p>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <tbody>
            <tr><th>From</th><td>Forest Churches &lt;info@forestchurches.co.uk&gt;</td></tr>
            <tr><th>SMTP server</th><td><code>smtp.ionos.co.uk</code>, port 587, STARTTLS when available</td></tr>
            <tr><th>SMTP sign-in</th><td>Key Vault <code>smtp-auth-username</code> / <code>smtp-auth-password</code> (read on every send)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Body</th><td>HTML only</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warn">
    <span class="docs-callout__title">Placeholders aren't HTML-encoded</span>
    <p>Values are inserted into the HTML as-is. The public contact forms put the visitor's name and message straight into an email sent from <em>info@forestchurches.co.uk</em>, so a visitor can add links or formatting. Treat contact-form emails with care, and see <a asp-page="/Docs/Admin/KnownIssues">Known issues</a>.</p>
</div>

